{
 "id": "2020-lavdas-organized",
 "name": "Organized Complexity and Landscape Preference",
 "description": "Do people prefer fractal, ornate and finely scaled forms over plain ones? Seven sets of edited Alpine scenes (Tyrol): a valley with a fractal or a rectilinear insert, a village with fractal or plain graphics and buildings, a stream with natural or simplified rocks and plants, and a path and a river bank with ornate pre-modern or plain modern buildings added. For each set, participants choose the picture they prefer and tick what made them choose it: most beautiful, most interesting, makes more sense, feels more familiar. Then gender, age, place of living and (optionally) country. Rebuilt from Lavdas & Schirpke (2020), PLOS ONE 15(6): e0235257 (n = 144 online respondents), using the paper's CC BY figures. Differences from the original study: (1) the images are the panels of the paper's Figs 1–7 at figure resolution (about 560 px wide), with the top strip holding the percentage labels cropped off; (2) the order of pictures within a set is randomised per participant (the paper does not report its layout); sets keep the paper's order 1–7; (3) English only (the original also ran in German and Italian); (4) the paper did not say whether a reason was required; here at least one is required; (5) the 'country' question is optional and free text.",
